data.content:
`SafeAreaEdges="None"` tells the page layout not to add padding for safe areas, but on **Android** you also need to enable **edge-to-edge mode** at the window level so the app actually draws behind the system bars. Without it, the OS still reserves space for the status/navigation bars.

**Fix — enable edge-to-edge on Android:**

In your `MauiProgram.cs` (or in a handler/lifecycle event):

```csharp
#if ANDROID
using Microsoft.Maui.LifecycleEvents;
using AndroidX.Activity;
using AndroidX.Activity.EdgeToEdge;
#endif

builder.ConfigureLifecycleEvents(events =>
{
#if ANDROID
    events.AddAndroid(android => android.OnCreate((activity, _) =>
    {
        if (activity is ComponentActivity componentActivity)
        {
            EdgeToEdgeKt.EnableEdgeToEdge(componentActivity);
        }
    }));
#endif
});
```

**Then on your page:**

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns:ios="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.PlatformConfiguration.iOSSpecific;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls"
             ios:Page.UseSafeArea="False">
    <Image Source="photo.jpg"
           Aspect="AspectFill"
           VerticalOptions="Fill"
           HorizontalOptions="Fill" />
</ContentPage>
```

**Summary of what's needed:**

| Platform | What to do |
|----------|-----------|
| Android  | Enable edge-to-edge via `EdgeToEdgeKt.EnableEdgeToEdge()` |
| iOS      | Set `ios:Page.UseSafeArea="False"` (or `SafeAreaEdges="None"` in .NET 10) |

The `SafeAreaEdges` property alone won't make Android draw behind system bars — that requires the platform-level opt-in.
